# If you prefer the allow list template instead of the deny list, see community template:
# https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/community/Golang/Go.AllowList.gitignore
#
# Binaries for programs and plugins
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# The bridge binary: `go build ./cmd/herdr-agent` drops a 13 MB executable here,
# and the deny list above only covers Windows and shared-library suffixes.
/herdr-agent

# Test binary, built with `go test -c`
*.test

# Code coverage profiles and other test artifacts
*.out
coverage.*
*.coverprofile
profile.cov

# Dependency directories (remove the comment below to include it)
# vendor/

# Go workspace file
go.work
go.work.sum

# env file
.env

# Editor/IDE
# .idea/
# .vscode/

# --- herdr-agent ---

# OS / editor
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
._*
.idea/
*.swp
*~

# Build output. `go build ./cmd/herdr-agent` drops the binary at the repo root,
# and the acceptance scripts build it as ./ha.
/herdr-agent
/ha
/bin/
/dist/
/build/
*.test
*.exe

# Coverage and profiles
*.out
coverage.*
*.prof

# Runtime and session state. None of this is source, and some of it is
# sensitive: the bridge's own state directory holds the chat-to-agent bindings
# and .env holds the Feishu app secret, while herdr's session.json describes
# whatever panes happened to be open on one machine.
.herdr-agent/
session.json
dedup.json
routes.json
selection.json
mirrors.json
*.pid
*.sock
*.log
log/

# Local bridge config produced from deploy/config.example.toml — it names the
# operator's Feishu open_id and notify chat id.
config.toml

# Claude Code local settings. Listed here rather than relying on a global
# ignore, so a fresh clone on another machine is protected too.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Orchestration scaffolding: the workflow scripts this was built with and the
# review findings they consumed. They embed absolute paths from the machine they
# ran on, and they are process, not product.
.workflows/

# Specs and acceptance scripts stay local: they quote absolute paths and
# document one operator's Feishu app setup. The measured facts they record
# (G1..G17) are what the code comments cite, so keep the local copy — the six
# load-bearing ones are summarised in README.
specs/
